WEBVTT

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:12.120
Welcome to the 7th Quarterly Report detailing the progress made by NASA's Ares Projects Office on America's next launch vehicle.

00:00:12.790 --> 00:00:17.410
Last quarter, we reported on ARIES-1's fabrication of the upper stage barrel panels.

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:20.860
and a new test stand being constructed for ARIES-1 use.

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:24.640
Testing continued from last quarter on wind tunnel models.

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:29.240
first stage parachutes, and preparations for J2X PowerPak testing.

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:33.380
Wind tunnel testing continued this quarter

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:36.720
A 1% scale model was tested at Langley and Boeing.

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:41.970
These evaluations are being used to develop the aerodynamic databases needed

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:43.790
by Ares One Engineers.

00:00:45.130 --> 00:00:49.410
A new series of tests began on an active damper system for wind tunnel use.

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:55.620
This system improves the data gained from wind tunnels by absorbing vibrations caused by the tunnel itself.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:59.880
In another Langley facility, work continued on the Ares I

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.400
X-Rigid Buffett model.

00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:07.090
This 3.5% scale model is equipped with more than 250 pressure sensors.

00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:14.290
Another model was tested to examine the heating and pressure of the Ares I first stage during re-entry.

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:19.950
The results of these tests will help engineers design the first stage thermal protection system.

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:26.480
A second drop test of the first stage main parachute was conducted in Yuma, Arizona this quarter.

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:29.980
The test put the parachute through 85% of its

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.060
maximum rated load and examined a different opening sequence, also called reefing, for

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:35.560
the parachute.

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:40.380
The third main parachute is being made for a cluster test in mid-April.

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:44.600
where the full array of three parachutes will be evaluated for first stage.

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:49.740
The parachutes are made of long strips of ribbon stitched together to form the final parachute.

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:59.960
Test firing of a solid rocket motor in November gave Ares engineers new data on the acoustic environment generated by the motor during firing.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:08.800
Other test objectives of this firing included testing a low-temperature O-ring material and radiometric measurements of the motor plume.

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:15.060
Testing continued in November on the Ascent Thrust Vector Controller for Ares 1X.

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:23.010
The latest hardware unit tested is identical in form and function to the final unit that will be used on the flight test in 2009.

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:27.820
This quarter, ATK completed assembly of a first-stage nozzle.

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:29.830
One of the notable advances

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:31.720
is a new carbon fiber rope.

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:34.650
used to reduce processing time and expense.

00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:37.570
over the methods currently used on the Space Shuttle.

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:42.530
The assembly allowed engineers to verify how all the components fit together.

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:44.480
and to test all their processes.

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:47.350
before hardware is built for hot fire testing.

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:53.380
ATK also began assembly this quarter on the first five-segment motors for the Ares I.

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:57.760
The new insulation material reported on last year is more eco-friendly.

00:02:58.090 --> 00:02:59.690
and safer to handle for workers.

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:01.140
It is also lighter.

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:04.650
which allows Ares engineers to increase propellant and performance.

00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:14.190
Upper stage hardware is also being manufactured now with the formation of the first gore panels for the liquid hydrogen tank dome.

00:03:14.710 --> 00:03:19.630
These panels are stretched, heat cured, and then stretched again into the needed shape.

00:03:19.790 --> 00:03:22.730
These initial panels are intended to test those processes.

00:03:23.130 --> 00:03:25.550
as well as the material properties of the panels.

00:03:26.770 --> 00:03:29.810
Evaluation and checkout of the new robotic weld

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.140
Tool at Marshall continued this quarter.

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:37.180
Preliminary testing is done on the smaller process development system before moving work

00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:39.260
onto the larger robotic weld tool.

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:44.060
A new vertical weld tool was built for use on Ares-1.

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:49.670
This tool allows upper stage manufacturing issues to be worked out at Marshall before

00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:52.890
production begins at Michoud Assembly Facility in Louisiana.

00:03:53.990 --> 00:03:57.250
Major components of the tool began arriving at Marshall in December.

00:03:57.640 --> 00:03:59.940
with system checkout expected in early spring.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:00.800
2008.

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:07.140
Engineers have begun bench-level pressure and leak valve testing using Heritage Saturn hardware.

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:13.500
Lessons learned from these tests will aid engineers in the design of the upper stage main propulsion system.

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:17.760
Testing continued this quarter on subscale diffuser hardware.

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:20.240
for the new A3 test stand.

00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:25.850
The diffuser will reduce pressure at the J2X nozzle to simulate high-altitude starts.

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:29.890
Work continues on the construction of test stand A3.

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:35.400
The concrete main foundation was poured in a continuous 14-hour operation in December.

00:04:36.350 --> 00:04:38.310
Nearby at Test Stand A1.

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:41.360
The J2X began PowerPak testing in December.

00:04:41.950 --> 00:04:45.690
Engineers did a complete status check of the facility and the power pack.

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:51.680
Coal flow testing was conducted by running the cryogenic fuels through the system without igniting them.

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:55.580
The gas generators were also tested with a helium spin start.

00:04:56.680 --> 00:05:00.000
Join us next quarter for progress reports on First Stage 10.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:01.120
test and modifications,

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:03.840
propellant loading of the solid rocket motor,

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:06.500
and hot fire testing of the J2X.

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:10.680
If you would like more information about ARIES plans and progress,

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:15.040
please visit us on the web at nasa.gov slash ARIES.
